            "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
            "body": "Thanks for the fix — the bug is real: `?` currently consumes a single *byte*, so `?.txt` doesn't match `é.txt`, and `[!a]` doesn't match `é` either.\r\n\r\nA couple of notes on how I'd prefer to see this done. Please treat these as a personal preference, not a requirement — happy to go with `utfR` if that's the direction the maintainers want.\r\n\r\n**Why the current code is byte-oriented.** The matcher is built on byte-level scanning on purpose: `notslash = m.P(1) - m.P'/'` is a charset difference, and the segment/star machinery leans on that — the `FIRST` optimization (`notslash * (notslash - m.P(F))^0`), the literal matcher, the star matcher. Since lpeg itself is byte-oriented, keeping the glob matcher byte-oriented is what lets those fast paths apply. The visible cost is that `*` can split a multi-byte character; that was a deliberate trade-off rather than an oversight, and I didn't want `?` and the negated class to be the one place where the unit silently changes.\r\n\r\n**What I'd suggest instead.** The file already contains a hand-written UTF-8 character matcher, `any_utf8`, used to parse character-class contents. Reusing that same matcher on the matching side keeps a single notion of \"character\" in the file, and keeps it expressible as bytes rather than as decoded codepoints:\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n-    patt = m.P(1) - patt --[[@as vim.lpeg.Pattern]]\r\n+    patt = any_utf8 - patt --[[@as vim.lpeg.Pattern]]\r\n```\r\n\r\n```diff\r\n-  Ques     = m.P'?' * m.Cc(notslash),\r\n+  Ques     = m.P'?' * m.Cc(any_utf8 - m.P'/'),\r\n```\r\n\r\nFor the record, the root cause is that `m.P(1) - patt` is a charset difference against a set that contains `utfR` ranges, so lpeg can't do a set difference and falls back to `Seq(Not patt, P(1))` — the \"not in class\" assertion is codepoint-level (`utfR`) while the match itself consumes one byte. `any_utf8 - patt` keeps the same assertion but consumes a whole character. This is visible without touching `?` at all: on current master `[!à-ê]` fails to match `中` even though `中` is outside the range, because the fallback eats one byte of the three.\r\n\r\nTwo smaller points in favour of this route:\r\n\r\n- `utfR` rejects overlong encodings, so with `any_utf8 = m.utfR(0, 0x10FFFF)` a pattern such as `[\\xe0\\x80\\x80]` stops parsing and `to_lpeg` raises \"Invalid glob\", where today it parses and simply doesn't match. The hand-written chain accepts it.\r\n- lpeg's utf-range first-set is a coarse approximation (bytes 0x00-0xF4), which gives up some of the choice optimization.\r\n\r\n**One behaviour change worth deciding on deliberately.** `?` on master matches any byte, including bytes that aren't valid UTF-8; after this change it matches only a well-formed sequence. So a filename containing a stray `0xFF` byte stops matching `?` (and `?.txt`). Filesystem paths are byte strings, so this is a real, if uncommon, difference. It is independent of which of the two spellings above we pick — both are strict about the sequence — but it should be a decision rather than a side effect.\r\n\r\n**One question I don't have an answer to.** What should `?` count as? minimatch counts UTF-16 code units, so `?` does not match an astral character; Python's fnmatch and Bun.Glob count code points. Both `utfR` and the hand-written chain count code points. Either is defensible — if code-unit semantics is wanted that's a different and larger change.\r\n\r\nAlso worth stating explicitly: `*` stays byte-oriented in all of the above. Making `?` codepoint-aware while `*` stays byte-based is a half-measure, so if the maintainers would rather keep the whole matcher byte-based, the alternative is to leave `?` as-is and document the behaviour. Not a blocker either way. Separately, and unrelated to this PR: `*?*?` doesn't match `abc` on current master either, so `*` followed by `?` is its own bug — I already have a fix for it on my side and plan to open a separate PR for it, so it shouldn't hold this one up.\r\n\r\n**Test coverage.** All the `?` cases in `glob_spec.lua` are ASCII (`?`, `??`, `a?c`, `?/?`, `/??`, `/?b`, `foo?bar`), which is why none of the above was caught. Adding a few non-ASCII ones (`?.txt` vs `é.txt`, `a?` vs `aé`, `[!a]` vs `é`) would pin down whichever semantics we settle on. Happy to send that along if useful.",
